  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B054",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks of unprecedented precision"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B035",
    "B018"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B054 remains the strongest combination of a distinct physical architecture, mature experimental evidence, and a prize-limited attribution. Magic-wavelength confinement addresses a central systematic problem while allowing the large atom numbers needed for exceptional clock performance. The stated 10^-18 regime has been reached repeatedly in independent laboratories, so this is not a proposal or a prospective technology. A citation confined to optical lattice clocks, rather than to all of frequency metrology or their possible future applications, gives Katori's conceptual contribution and Ye's decisive experimental and metrological development a coherent and testable credit line.",
    "case_against": "The principal reservation remains serious: optical lattice clocks are also an evolving collective metrological programme. Before a final award, the historical record must establish that the cited architecture and its defining realization can be separated from broader optical-frequency, laser, and clock-comparison advances, and that Katori and Ye are the exact prize-limited attribution.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that the magic-wavelength lattice is a distinct physical solution, whose independently reproduced 10^-18 performance makes lattice clocks more than an incremental instrumentation advance.",
        "response": "I agree, particularly with keeping the citation centered on the architecture rather than claiming all downstream uses. The experimental criterion is decisive for me: repeatedly obtaining this precision with independently developed clocks shows that the original control principle has become a reliable way of observing frequency and relativistic effects at a new level. That maturity is sufficient without awaiting any formal change to the definition of the second."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson argues that aberration correction transformed electron microscopy into a broadly enabling atomic-scale observational capability, while warning that its credit spans several correction designs and instrument-building routes.",
        "response": "I continue to regard B035 as an outstanding alternative because it changed what can be measured in matter. But Olsson's qualification is material, not cosmetic: a final prize must identify an exact conceptual and practical chain among several intertwined routes. B054 also needs archival scrutiny, yet its proposed two-person line can be more narrowly tied to the lattice-clock principle and defining development."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth argues that the recollision model supplied a simple, repeatedly tested microscopic mechanism for high-harmonic generation and attosecond science, distinct from merely later applications.",
        "response": "I accept the intellectual force and experimental validation of B018, and it remains a strong alternative. My concern is not its scientific merit but prize boundary: the proposed theoretical recognition must be demonstrably separable from the closely related experimental attosecond advances already given major recognition. The lattice-clock achievement has a more operationally delimited object—the magic-wavelength clock architecture—and a more directly auditable record of reproducible performance."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival evidence supports isolating Katori's and Ye's decisive contributions from indispensable wider developments in optical-frequency metrology.",
      "Whether the final citation can remain confined to optical lattice clocks without implying credit for all later clock applications and technical advances.",
      "Whether the proposed B035 attribution can be justified exactly across distinct correction theories and practical implementations."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No conflict with the proposed laureates is apparent from the supplied profile. I work in the Swedish quantum-technology environment, including WACQT, which overlaps institutionally with fellow member Göran Johansson's home institution, but no specific collaboration or connection to the B054 laureates is identified."
  }
}
